    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d284">

                <pb facs="0518" n="503" sameAs="frus:frus1949v04/pg503"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">760H.658/4–1449: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Italy</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Dunn</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the
                            Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Rome</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1949-04-14T19:00:00+01:00">April 14, 1949—7
                            p. m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1094. Italian Foreign Office states fisheries convention with Yugoslavia signed
                    Belgrade April 13 accompanied by exchange notes in <pb facs="0519" n="504"
                        xml:id="pg504"/> general terms relative to interest of Italians and
                    Yugoslavs to negotiate expanded trade agreement without delay.<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d284-f1"/> Italians did not make commitment re specific volume of
                    trade. (ReEmbtel 1075 to Department, Paris 93, Belgrade 43, April 12.<anchor
                        n="2" xml:id="d284-f2"/>) Romsa equipment<anchor n="3" xml:id="d284-f3"/>
                    transferred to Yugoslavia without payment. Agreement transferring naval vessels,
                    Article 57 Treaty of Peace signed Rome today.<anchor n="4" xml:id="d284-f4"/>
                    Agreement Italian property nationalized by Yugoslavia and property of optants in
                    ceded territory, annex XV Treaty of Peace expected be signed Belgrade today.</p>
                <p>Sent Department 1094, repeated Belgrade 46, Paris 95.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Dunn</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d284-f1" n="1" xml:id="d284-f1-content">
                <p>In Rome despatch No. 573 of April 14, 1949, not printed, Byington forwarded the
                    full French text of the agreement in 13 articles. (860H.628/4–2649)</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d284-f2" n="2" xml:id="d284-f2-content">
                <p>In this telegram, not printed, Dunn reported that the terms of the fisheries
                    agreement were acceptable to the Italians, but that signature was delayed
                    because of last-minute insistence by the Yugoslavs that the Italian delegate
                    spell out the increased volume of trade to be expected under the trade agreement
                    being negotiated (760H.658/4–1249).</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d284-f3" n="3" xml:id="d284-f3-content">
                <p>Apparently refers to oil refinery equipment.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d284-f4" n="4" xml:id="d284-f4-content">
                <p>For documentation on the negotiations leading to the naval agreement, see <ref
                        target="frus1948v03#pg_969">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, 1948, vol. <hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">iii</hi>, pp. 969</ref> ff.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
